pages=[]
pages.append(dict(file='index.html', short='Understand the shape', title='Loft your camera mount.', kicker='01 / SEE THE IDEA', lead='Blend a footprint on the base into a smaller profile on the tilted camera plate. A loft builds the material between them.', content=f'''
<div class="hero-grid"><div class="figure hero-figure"><img src="assets/mount.svg" alt="Proposed loft connecting a teal foot profile to an orange camera plate profile"><div class="figure-bar"><span><i class="dot teal"></i>A / foot</span><span><i class="dot orange"></i>B / camera plate</span><span>Proposed geometry</span></div></div><aside class="recipe"><span class="eyebrow">THE RECIPE</span><div class="recipe-line"><b>2</b><span>closed profiles<br><small>on separate faces</small></span></div><div class="recipe-line"><b>1</b><span>solid loft<br><small>added to Camera mount</small></span></div><p>Start with simple rectangles. Add shape controls only after the basic loft works.</p><a class="button" href="profiles.html">Make the profiles <span>→</span></a></aside></div>
<div class="section-heading"><span class="eyebrow">YOUR STARTING POINT</span><h2>Inside your actual document</h2><p>I inspected <b>Drivetrain → Drivetrain Parts Studio</b> on October 29, 2026. These are live document references; the colored teaching diagrams are schematic.</p></div>
<div class="split"><figure class="reference"><img src="assets/onshape-mount.jpg" alt="Actual Onshape document showing the Limelight 4 camera, tilted mount plate, foot, and feature tree" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Your existing camera mount, before this tutorial.</figcaption></figure><div class="stack"><div class="card"><span class="mini-number">01</span><h3>Find the correct items</h3><ul class="clean-list"><li><b>Camera mount (9)</b> is a feature folder.</li><li><b>Camera mount</b> under Parts is the solid to merge into.</li><li><b>Camera loft</b> is currently a sketch, not a Loft feature.</li></ul></div><div class="card"><span class="mini-number">02</span><h3>Reuse your starting sketch</h3><p>Its sketch plane is <b>Face of Extrude 3</b>. Inspect its closed regions before reusing it as profile B. The name alone does not create a loft.</p><p>The sketch is under-defined. A closed under-defined sketch can still loft; dimensions and constraints make it more reliable.</p></div><div class="note"><b>Our worked example</b><p>A reinforcing transition on the mount side of the plate. Hide the camera to find that side. Pick a clear area away from screws, fins, connectors, and the swerve hardware.</p></div></div></div>
<div class="section-heading"><span class="eyebrow">WHY A LOFT?</span><h2>The sections can change shape and angle.</h2></div><div class="three"><div class="card"><div class="icon-line">▱ → ▱</div><h3>Extrude</h3><p>Pushes one profile in a straight direction. Good when the cross-section stays the same.</p></div><div class="card accented"><div class="icon-line">▱ ↗ ▰</div><h3>Loft</h3><p>Transitions between profiles. Useful here because the foot and plate face different directions.</p></div><div class="card"><div class="icon-line">◯ ⤳</div><h3>Sweep</h3><p>Carries a profile along a path. Useful for a tube or cable route.</p></div></div>
<label class="check"><input type="checkbox" data-check="idea"><span>I can point to the foot, the tilted plate, and the clear space where my loft will go.</span></label>
'''))
pages.append(dict(file='profiles.html', short='Draw two profiles', title='Give the loft two ends.', kicker='02 / DRAW THE PROFILES', lead='Draw one closed region on the foot and one on the plate. Keep them separated and use four sides on both for your first attempt.', content='''
<div class="figure"><img src="assets/profiles.svg" alt="A rectangular profile on the foot and a separate rectangular profile on the tilted camera plate"><div class="figure-bar"><span><i class="dot teal"></i>Profile A = start</span><span><i class="dot orange"></i>Profile B = end</span><span>Example sizes, not measured</span></div></div>
<div class="note"><b>Choose placement before dimensions.</b><p>The rectangles should fit fully on solid mount material, and the volume between them should stay clear of hardware. The 26 × 8 mm and 18 × 6 mm dimensions are practice values, not validated sizes for your mount. Enter units explicitly if your document uses inches.</p></div>
<div class="split"><div class="card steps"><span class="profile-tag teal-tag">A / FOOT</span><h2>Create the base profile</h2><ol><li><b>Get a clear view.</b> In Parts, right-click Camera mount → Zoom to selection. Hide the camera composite if it blocks the mount side.</li><li><b>Select a flat top face of the foot.</b> Choose Sketch. The face itself supplies the sketch plane; an offset plane is not required.</li><li><b>Draw a corner rectangle</b> inside the usable foot area. Start with a small footprint away from bolt holes and tool access.</li><li><b>Size and locate it.</b> Use Dimension for width, depth, and distances to stable edges. Keep construction geometry out of the loop.</li><li><b>Finish the sketch</b> with the green check. Rename it <em>Loft A — foot</em>.</li></ol><label class="check"><input type="checkbox" data-check="profile-a"><span>I finished one closed region on the foot.</span></label></div>
<div class="card steps"><span class="profile-tag orange-tag">B / CAMERA PLATE</span><h2>Inspect “Camera loft”</h2><ol><li><b>Right-click Camera loft → Edit.</b> Its inspected plane is Face of Extrude 3. Use a normal view to see its outline clearly.</li><li><b>Check that it is the intended support-side face.</b> If it is the wrong face, cancel and create a separate sketch on the correct mount face.</li><li><b>Reuse one suitable closed rectangle.</b> If none exists, draw a small rectangle on this face, above and separate from profile A. Do not select the entire plate with its bolt holes.</li><li><b>Dimension the region and its position.</b> Keep it inside the plate and aligned with A along the mount's width. Use matching corner order on both profiles.</li><li><b>Finish the sketch.</b> Keep the name Camera loft or rename it <em>Loft B — plate</em> so you recognize it.</li></ol><label class="check"><input type="checkbox" data-check="profile-b"><span>I finished one closed region on the plate.</span></label></div></div>
<div class="split"><figure class="reference"><img src="assets/onshape-profile.jpg" loading="lazy" alt="Normal view of the actual plate with the Camera loft sketch selected"><figcaption>Normal view from your document. Confirm the region in sketch edit mode; this view alone does not prove that the loop is closed.</figcaption></figure><div class="card"><span class="eyebrow">BEFORE YOU OPEN LOFT</span><h2>Two regions, two planes</h2><p>Hover inside each loop. You should be able to identify a filled sketch region, not only individual lines. If the mount face obscures the sketch, temporarily hide the Camera mount part while checking the sketch regions.</p><ul><li>Close any tiny gaps with Coincident constraints.</li><li>Remove duplicate or overlapping sketch segments.</li><li>Keep the profiles physically separate; avoid crossing loops.</li><li>Each profile must contact its mount face over an area, not only touch at a point or edge.</li></ul><p><b>No plane button needed?</b> Correct. Existing flat faces can be sketch planes. Use Plane → Offset only when you need a profile floating away from a face.</p></div></div>
'''))
pages.append(dict(file='loft.html', short='Build the loft', title='Connect A to B.', kicker='03 / CREATE THE SOLID', lead='Finish sketch editing, open Loft, and choose the two filled regions in order. Then add the preview to the Camera mount part.', content=f'''
<div class="loft-lab"><div class="figure">{svg('Interactive teaching model: select A and then B to preview a loft',select)}<div class="figure-bar"><span>Teaching model</span><span>Click A, then B</span><span>Not a CAD solver</span></div></div><div class="mock-dialog"><span class="eyebrow">TRY THE SELECTION ORDER</span><h2>Loft</h2><div class="pills"><span class="selected">Solid</span><span>Surface</span><span>Thin</span></div><div class="pills"><span>New</span><span class="selected">Add</span><span>Remove</span></div><div class="field-label">Profiles</div><div class="profile-list" aria-live="polite"><span id="selection-a">① Select A / foot</span><span id="selection-b">② Select B / plate</span></div><div class="selection-buttons"><button type="button" data-select="a">Select A</button><button type="button" data-select="b">Select B</button><button type="button" id="selection-reset">Reset</button></div><p id="selection-feedback" role="status">Start by selecting the teal region A.</p><div class="dialog-divider"></div><dl><dt>Start condition</dt><dd>None</dd><dt>End condition</dt><dd>None</dd><dt>Guides / Path</dt><dd>Off</dd><dt>Merge scope</dt><dd>Camera mount</dd></dl><p class="small">This panel demonstrates the choices. Make the actual selections in Onshape.</p></div></div>
<div class="section-heading"><span class="eyebrow">NOW IN ONSHAPE</span><h2>Build your first working version</h2></div>
<div class="two"><div class="card steps"><span class="mini-number">01</span><h3>Open the feature</h3><p>Finish all sketches. Click <b>Search tools</b> in the top toolbar, search for <b>Loft</b>, and open it. Choose <b>Solid</b>, then <b>Add</b>.</p><p>Make sure both sketch regions are visible. Onshape remembers its last creation type, so check Solid explicitly.</p></div><div class="card steps"><span class="mini-number">02</span><h3>Select the profile interiors</h3><p>Activate the <b>Profiles</b> field. Click inside <b>A / foot</b>, then inside <b>B / plate</b>. Look for <b>two separate profile entries</b> in that order.</p><p>If the whole underlying face highlights, temporarily hide Camera mount and select the sketch region. Restore the part before checking the merge.</p></div><div class="card steps"><span class="mini-number">03</span><h3>Keep the controls simple</h3><p>Start and end conditions: <b>None</b>. Leave <b>Guides and continuity</b> and <b>Path</b> off. A basic two-profile loft can have straight sides; curvature controls are optional.</p><p>Rotate the preview. It should bridge the intended gap with no pinching or sideways twist. If corners cross, see the next page.</p></div><div class="card steps"><span class="mini-number">04</span><h3>Merge and accept</h3><p>Set <b>Merge scope → Camera mount</b>. If <b>Merge with all</b> is enabled, turn it off and select that part explicitly. Verify the loft joins the support over a face area.</p><p>Accept with the green check. Rename the feature <b>Camera support loft</b>. You should still have one Camera mount solid, with the support added.</p></div></div>
<div class="note"><b>If Add fails, separate the two questions.</b><p>Temporarily switch to <b>New</b> to see whether the shape can be generated. If New works, investigate contact and merge scope. Switch back to Add before accepting the final support. A detached New part is only a diagnostic preview.</p></div>
<label class="check"><input type="checkbox" data-check="preview"><span>The preview joins A to B with no crossed connections or pinched faces.</span></label><label class="check"><input type="checkbox" data-check="merge"><span>I accepted the loft with Add and merged it into Camera mount.</span></label>
'''))
pages.append(dict(file='check.html', short='Fix & finish', title='Make the result make sense.', kicker='04 / CHECK & REFINE', lead='A successful feature is the first checkpoint. Now check the shape, the merge, and the space needed by the camera and its hardware.', content=f'''
<div class="figure twist-figure">{svg('Compare correctly matched corners with crossed loft connections',twist)}<div class="figure-bar"><div class="toggle-group"><button type="button" data-twist="good" aria-pressed="true">Correct connections</button><button type="button" data-twist="bad" aria-pressed="false">Crossed connections</button></div><span>Try both</span></div></div>
<div class="section-heading"><span class="eyebrow">WHEN SOMETHING GOES WRONG</span><h2>Match the symptom to the fix</h2></div>
<div class="fixes">
<details open><summary>No region to click</summary><div><p>Your sketch may have a gap, a duplicate segment, or construction-only geometry. Edit that sketch, zoom to every corner, and close the loop with Coincident constraints. Finish the sketch and select its interior.</p><p>If a solid face covers the region, hide the part temporarily. Sketch line color indicates constraint state; it does not by itself prove that a region is closed.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>The entire plate or its holes are selected</summary><div><p>Remove that selection from Profiles using its ×. Hide the mount part temporarily and select only the rectangle's sketch region. Restore the part to inspect the result. The plate outline and bolt holes are not the two ends of this practice support.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>The loft twists or pinches</summary><div><p>Check for two simple four-sided profiles first. Enable <b>Connections</b> in Loft. Pair corresponding corners: left with left, right with right, following the intended sides.</p><p>Onshape displays automatic connections in magenta. For explicit match connections, each entry contains a pair of vertices or edges, one from each profile. Add another pair if needed. Remove conflicting matches and keep profiles in travel order.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>New works, but Add fails</summary><div><p>The shape is probably valid, but its contact or merge is not. Set Merge scope to <b>Camera mount</b>. Check the real gap in a side view or section view: the loft must meet the mount over a finite area. A point or edge contact can create invalid zero-thickness geometry.</p><p>Keep sketches attached to the intended faces. Move or resize a profile if the loft only grazes the mount. Return to Add before accepting.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>The loft bulges or intersects hardware</summary><div><p>Reset start/end conditions to None. Reduce the profile sizes or move them into a clear zone. A loft follows the selected profiles, not the surrounding hardware; it will not automatically route around screw heads, connectors, or the swerve module.</p><p>If you later need a controlled side boundary, add a guide that intersects every profile boundary. Use Pierce or Coincident constraints to enforce the intersections. Add one control at a time.</p></div></details>
<details><summary>I want a smoother transition</summary><div><p>After the basic loft works, edit it and try <b>Normal to profile</b> at one end. That makes the loft's tangent direction align with that profile's normal. Adjust Magnitude gently and watch for bulges.</p><p><b>Match tangent</b> and <b>Match curvature</b> depend on suitable adjacent model faces. They are not general-purpose smoothing switches. For a rounded root, a small Fillet after the loft may be easier; verify that the fillet still leaves adequate material.</p></div></details>
</div>
<div class="section-heading"><span class="eyebrow">BEFORE YOU CALL IT FINISHED</span><h2>Orbit around the whole mount.</h2><p>Show the camera and surrounding parts again. Use a side view and a section view if contact or internal clearance is hard to see.</p></div>
<div class="two"><div class="card"><h3>Geometry</h3><label class="check"><input type="checkbox" data-check="solid"><span>The support is part of the Camera mount solid, with no detached sliver or error icon.</span></label><label class="check"><input type="checkbox" data-check="thickness"><span>The loft has material throughout; its faces do not pinch down to zero thickness.</span></label></div><div class="card"><h3>Access & fit</h3><label class="check"><input type="checkbox" data-check="hardware"><span>Screws, driver/socket access, and the swerve hardware have room.</span></label><label class="check"><input type="checkbox" data-check="camera"><span>The camera body, cooling fins, cable bend, and connectors have clearance.</span></label></div></div>
<div class="finish"><div><span class="eyebrow">YOU HAVE THE CORE WORKFLOW</span><h2>Sketch A → sketch B → Loft → Add.</h2><p>These pages teach the modeling workflow. Example dimensions and diagrams are not a structural or manufacturing validation of your mount.</p></div><a href="{DOC}" class="button" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Back to your document ↗</a></div>
<div class="sources"><b>References</b><p>Document and feature names were inspected in your linked Part Studio on October 29, 2026. Loft controls, profile order, connections, and merge behavior were checked against <a href="{HELP}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Onshape's official Loft help</a>. No CAD geometry was changed while preparing this guide.</p></div>
'''))

def nav(current):
    return ''.join(f'<a class="nav-step {"active" if i==current else ""}" href="{p["file"]}" {"aria-current=page" if i==current else ""}><span class="nav-num">0{i+1}</span><span>{p["short"]}</span><span class="nav-arrow">↗</span></a>' for i,p in enumerate(pages))

def header(p):
    return f'<header class="page-heading"><div class="eyebrow">{p["kicker"]}</div><h1>{p["title"]}</h1><p class="lead">{p["lead"]}</p></header>'

for i,p in enumerate(pages):
    previous = f'<a class="back" href="{pages[i-1]["file"]}">← {pages[i-1]["short"]}</a>' if i else '<span class="small">Work alongside your Onshape tab.</span>'
    following = f'<a class="button" href="{pages[i+1]["file"]}">Next: {pages[i+1]["short"]} →</a>' if i<3 else '<a class="button secondary" href="index.html">Review from the start ↺</a>'
    content=f'''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="theme-color" content="#112d3e"><title>{p['short']} · Camera Loft Guide</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="guide.css"><script src="guide.js" defer></script></head><body data-page="{i}"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to lesson</a><aside class="sidebar"><a href="index.html" class="brand"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m7 30 18 4 9-18-14-4Z M7 30 14 7 28 10 34 16 M14 7 20 12 M25 34 28 10" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/></svg><span>CAMERA<br><strong>LOFT LAB</strong></span></a><div class="sidebar-label">ONSHAPE / FIELD GUIDE</div><nav aria-label="Lessons">{nav(i)}</nav><div class="sidebar-bottom"><div class="progress-caption"><span>Your checkpoints</span><span data-progress-count>0 / 9</span></div><div class="progress-track"><div data-progress-bar></div></div><p class="small">Tick steps as you finish them.<br>Progress saves in this browser.</p><a href="{DOC}" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="doc-link">Open your Onshape doc ↗</a><a href="print.html" class="quiet-link">All lessons / print view</a><button type="button" id="reset-progress" class="text-button">Reset checkpoints</button></div></aside><main id="main"><div class="topline"><span>DRIVETRAIN / LIMELIGHT 4</span><span>LESSON 0{i+1} OF 04</span></div>{header(p)}{p['content']}<footer class="page-nav">{previous}{following}</footer><div class="page-foot">CAMERA LOFT LAB <span>Made for your Drivetrain Parts Studio · Diagrams not to scale</span></div></main></body></html>'''
    (ROOT/p['file']).write_text(content,encoding='utf-8')

print_content=''.join(f'<section class="print-lesson">{header(p)}{p["content"]}</section>' for p in pages)
(ROOT/'print.html').write_text(f'''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>All lessons · Camera Loft Guide</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="guide.css"><script src="guide.js" defer></script></head><body class="print-view"><main><div class="print-controls"><a href="index.html">← Interactive guide</a><button class="button" type="button" id="print-guide">Print / save PDF</button></div>{print_content}</main></body></html>''',encoding='utf-8')
print('Built 4 lesson pages and print.html')
